Here's the complete migration from ASP.NET Framework to ASP.NET Core (.NET 8).

## Pattern Classification

Your code has two patterns from the skill guide:

| Location | Pattern | Issue |
|----------|---------|-------|
| `Response.End()` | ASP.NET request termination | Calls `Thread.Abort` internally — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8 |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | ASP.NET request termination | The `true` (endResponse) parameter calls `Thread.Abort` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | ThreadAbortException as control flow | `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown in .NET 8 — dead code |

## Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core / .NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST: /Report/Export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // In ASP.NET Core, return a result object instead of writing
        // directly to the response. No Response.End() needed — the
        // framework handles the response lifecycle.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    // POST: /Report/Redirect
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Return a redirect result — no Thread.Abort, no endResponse.
            // The framework short-circuits the pipeline automatically.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No catch (ThreadAbortException) — it is never thrown in .NET 8.
        // That catch block was only needed because Response.Redirect(url, true)
        // used Thread.Abort as a control-flow mechanism.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

### 1. `Response.End()` → just return
ASP.NET Core doesn't use `Response.End()`. Return an `IActionResult` and the framework writes the response and completes the request. For file downloads, `File()` sets content-type, content-disposition, and body in one call.

### 2. `Response.Redirect(url, true)` → `return Redirect(url)`
The `true` parameter existed to abort the thread and prevent code after the redirect from executing. In ASP.NET Core, `return Redirect(...)` exits the action method naturally — no abort needed.

### 3. Remove `catch (ThreadAbortException)`
This catch block was a workaround for `Response.Redirect(url, true)` throwing `ThreadAbortException` as control flow. Since `Redirect()` returns normally, the exception is never thrown. **Keeping this catch block is dead code** and would mask future bugs if a real `ThreadAbortException`-like issue occurred.

### 4. Null-safety on `User.Identity`
In ASP.NET Core, `User.Identity` can be `null` if no authentication middleware is configured. The `?.` and `?? true` guard against a `NullReferenceException`.

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Writing to `Response.Body` then returning an `IActionResult` | ASP.NET Core throws — pick one or the other, not both |
| Calling `Response.Redirect()` without `return` | Unlike Framework, execution continues past the call. Always `return Redirect(...)` or code below will run |
| Leaving `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Dead code in .NET 8 — `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown. Remove it to avoid confusion |
| Using `HttpContext.Response.End()` | This method doesn't exist in ASP.NET Core — there is no equivalent. Return from the action instead |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` for long-running requests | If you add timeout support, wrap `CancellationTokenSource` in `using`. Leaking it causes timer leaks |

## If You Have Long-Running Request Work

Use `HttpContext.RequestAborted` (the ASP.NET Core equivalent of client-disconnect detection):

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> LongReport(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted
    // to the cancellationToken parameter
    var data = await GenerateLargeReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    return File(data, "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This replaces the old pattern where `Response.End()` / `Thread.Abort` would kill processing if the client disconnected.
